{
  "id": "echo-state-network",
  "title": "Echo State Network (Liquid State Machine)",
  "tags": [
    "reservoir computing",
    "recurrent",
    "temporal memory"
  ],
  "references": [
    "Jaeger (2001) The 'echo state' approach to analysing and training recurrent neural networks. GMD Report 148.",
    "Maass, Natschläger & Markram (2002) Real-time computing without stable states. Neural Computation."
  ],
  "network": {
    "version": 1,
    "name": "Echo State Network (spiking reservoir)",
    "description": "A fixed, randomly connected recurrent 'reservoir' of 400 spiking neurons (80% excitatory, 20% inhibitory) is driven by a repeating temporal sequence. The reservoir transforms the input into a rich, high-dimensional trajectory that 'echoes' recent history; only the connections to a small readout are plastic.",
    "biology": "Reservoir computing (Jaeger's echo state networks and Maass's liquid state machines) proposes that generic cortical microcircuits act as a fading memory of their inputs, and that downstream areas only need to learn simple readouts. The spiking version shown here is the liquid state machine; the balance of excitation and inhibition keeps the reservoir at the edge of chaos.",
